-- title:
--   Étale near a point from flat unramified stalk map
-- statement:
--   Let $f\colon U \to S$ be a morphism of schemes which is locally of finite presentation, and let $x$ be a point of $U$, with image $f(x)$ in $S$. Three conditions at $x$ are assumed: the induced homomorphism of stalks $f^{\sharp}_x\colon \mathcal{O}_{S,f(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{U,x}$ (the underlying ring map of `f.stalkMap x`) is flat; the ideal generated by the image under this homomorphism of the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{S,f(x)}$ is exactly the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{U,x}$, i.e. $\mathfrak{m}_{f(x)}\mathcal{O}_{U,x} = \mathfrak{m}_x$; and the induced morphism of residue fields $\kappa(f(x)) \to \kappa(x)$, namely `f.residueFieldMap x`, is an isomorphism. The conclusion is that $f$ is étale on some open neighbourhood of $x$: there exists an open subscheme $V$ of $U$ with $x \in V$ such that the composite of the open immersion $V \hookrightarrow U$ with $f$, i.e. the restriction $f|_V \colon V \to S$, is an étale morphism of schemes. Note that flatness is assumed only for the single local homomorphism of stalks at $x$, not for $f$ over an open set.
--
--   This is the pointwise form of the criterion that a morphism locally of finite presentation which is flat and unramified at a point is étale in a neighbourhood of that point (EGA IV, 17.4.1 and 17.6.1–17.6.3), the two conditions on the maximal ideal and the residue field expressing unramifiedness of the stalk map. It is used in the construction of Deligne–Rapoport models of modular curves, where étale charts are produced from stalkwise flatness and triviality of the residue extension, and in the verification that the relevant stalks are integrally closed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_exists_etale_nhd_of_flat_stalkMap_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq_of_isIso_residueFieldMap.lean

